  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1212 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nSaturday through Tuesday:\n\nThe cut off low to our south in the southern Plains will\ncontinue east toward the Mid Atlantic over the weekend as upper\nlevel ridging moves through the Great Lakes Region. Sfc high\npressure will be slowly descending down from Canada and should\nalso settle across the Great Lakes Region. As the sfc high moves\nsouth, colder air will descend with it bringing in a period of\ncooler weather. Day time highs are expected to be in the lower\nto upper 40s (warmer temps in the low 50s along the lake).\nOvernight lows expected to fall into the mid 20s to upper 30s\n(again warmer temps found near the lake). \n\nAs the high pressure moves in, winds will turn to northeasterly\nSaturday pushing any remaining lake effect precipitation toward\nMichigan. Dry weather is then expected to persist into mid next\nweek. The upper level ridging will remain in place with the sfc\nhigh very slowly ambling east into Wednesday. Under the high, \nwe can expect sunny and overall quiet conditions. As the high \nshifts east of the state early next week temperatures, should \nslightly increase coming out of the chilly weekend. \n\nPatterson",
